= Introduction <sec:intro>

Databases, file systems, and operating systems serialize access to their most contended state through locks @redb @upscaledb. Under load, a lock is therefore also a _scheduler_: at every hand-off it decides whose critical section runs next. Patel et al. showed that the usual notion of lock fairness, granting the lock in FIFO order, is the wrong goal @patel2020scl. When critical sections differ in length, a thread that holds the lock longer on every turn receives more of the machine; they called this _scheduler subversion_ and proposed _lock usage fairness_ in its place. The effect is easy to reproduce: when 1-record and 64-record writers share redb's single-writer lock, MCS @mellorcrummey1991mcs reaches a Jain index @jain1984fairness of only 0.85--0.86 over time spent in the critical section. The problem is well understood. This paper is about what it _costs_ to solve it.

Fairness is costly because of how a lock works. A thread acquires the lock and runs its own critical section, so the protected data lives wherever the last holder ran, and a fair hand-off to a thread on another core forces the data to follow. The tighter the fairness, the more often this migration is paid: in redb, MCS incurs 93--126 cross-core cache transfers per transaction. NUMA-aware queue locks such as CNA @dice2019cna and ShflLock @kashyap2019shfllock reorder waiters to make each transfer cheaper, but the data still moves on every hand-off @gupta2023tclocks, and chiplet processors, which split a socket into dies with separate last-level caches @naffziger2021chiplet, make each move costlier even within a socket.

Practitioners therefore relax fairness. Rust's `parking_lot` `Mutex` lets a releasing thread reacquire the lock and forces a fair unlock only about every 0.5 ms, while its strictly FIFO `FairMutex` is documented as "generally slower, but sometimes needed" @parkinglot; u-SCL grants each thread a _lock slice_ during which it may reacquire the lock freely @patel2020scl. Such _eventually fair_ locks buy locality by letting one thread keep the lock for longer, and so give up either short-term fairness or, in u-SCL's case, work conservation: its fairness target counts idle time as opportunity, so the lock can sit reserved for a slice owner that is running non-critical code while others wait. In each case fairness and locality are traded against each other.

This trade-off rests on an assumption so basic that it usually goes unstated: the thread that is served is the thread that executes. _Delegation_ locks are a special kind of lock that breaks it. In flat combining @hendler2010fc and its successors, up to TCLocks @gupta2023tclocks, threads publish their critical sections as requests and a single _combiner_ executes them, so the data stays on one core no matter whose request runs. Existing delegation locks use this separation for speed and transparency but serve requests in queue order, and so remain as usage-unfair as FIFO locks. Once execution no longer follows the lock, however, choosing _whose_ request runs next becomes a pure scheduling decision, and the combiner, which executes every request, can measure each one's cost for the price of two timestamp reads.

We build on this observation with FC-PQ, a usage-fair delegation lock. Its combiner serves pending requests in order of least accumulated critical-section time, in the spirit of virtual-time fair queueing @demers1989wfq, and a fast path lets an uncontended thread execute directly. FC-PQ pursues SCL's goal in work-conserving form: whenever a request is pending, the combiner runs one. To reason about when this pays, we develop an analysis framework that splits the price of fairness into accounting, migration, idleness, and redistribution, and shows that delegation leaves mainly the first and last.

We evaluate FC-PQ inside redb and UpScaleDB, charging service to the requesting thread, with the CPU clock fixed. On redb, FC-PQ matches u-SCL's fairness (0.94--0.995) at the native mutex's throughput, with 10--18 cross-core transfers per transaction where MCS incurs 93--126. Our prototype's waiters spin, which costs CPU time and limits it to no more threads than cores; this is an implementation choice, not a property of usage-fair delegation, since waiting and serving are independent and delegation locks can park their waiters @gupta2023tclocks. On redb's back-to-back writers FC-PQ only ties u-SCL, whose slices waste nothing when threads never leave the lock. We expect FC-PQ to pull ahead when threads do real work between critical sections and when the protected data is large; Section 8 states these predictions.

This paper makes the following contributions:

- We show that the cost of lock usage fairness comes from coupling who is served with where the critical section runs, which forces a fair lock to pay in migration or, if it reserves the lock, in idleness.
- We give an analysis framework that decomposes the price of fairness and predicts when delegation lowers it.
- We design FC-PQ, a work-conserving, usage-fair delegation lock with a fast path proven sound for the uncontended case.
- We evaluate FC-PQ in redb and UpScaleDB with service charged to the requester and a fixed CPU clock, and report which costs of fairness delegation removes and which remain.

Section 2 reviews lock usage fairness; Section 3 develops the analysis framework; Section 4 presents FC-PQ; Section 5 describes our methodology; Sections 6 and 7 evaluate redb and UpScaleDB; Section 8 discusses limitations and predictions; Section 9 covers related work.
